-- title:
--   Residues of j_N-a and j_{Nq}-a on both prolongations
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a natural number $N\neq 0$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$; fix further a datum `data` consisting of a monic $\Phi\in\mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the level-$q$ $q$-expansion, a witness `hKr` that its bivariate reduction modulo $q$ equals $(C(X)^q-X)(C(X)-X^q)$, witnesses `hα`, `hβ` that the two Hecke correspondence maps `heckeAlphaBar`, `heckeBetaBar` for level $N$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ are integral ring homomorphisms, a place specialisation $P$ of these data, and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$, carrying two regular prolongations `R.R₁`, `R.R₂` of $A$ to $\overline{\mathbb Q}(X(Nq))$-base-changed Laurent series with residue maps `R.residue₁`, `R.residue₂` into `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, the subfield of $k$-Laurent series generated by `jqModC k` and `jqNModC k N`. Assume $q\nmid N$ and let $a\in A$. Consider in `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` the two elements obtained by pushing $j_q$ expanded in $\mathfrak q^N$, respectively in $\mathfrak q^{Nq}$, along the coefficient embedding of $\mathbb Q$ into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, each minus the image of $a$. The assertion is that both elements lie in the integers of `R.R₁` and in the integers of `R.R₂`, and that, writing $\tilde\jmath_N$ for `jNGeomGen k N` and $\bar a=\mathrm{red}(a)$, their residues are $\mathrm{residue₁}(j(\mathfrak q^N)-a)=\tilde\jmath_N-\bar a$, $\mathrm{residue₂}(j(\mathfrak q^N)-a)=\tilde\jmath_N^{\,q}-\bar a$, $\mathrm{residue₁}(j(\mathfrak q^{Nq})-a)=\tilde\jmath_N^{\,q}-\bar a$ and $\mathrm{residue₂}(j(\mathfrak q^{Nq})-a)=\tilde\jmath_N-\bar a$.
--
--   This is the second-coordinate companion of the corresponding statement for the $j$-coordinate: it expresses, in the valuation-theoretic language of prolongation tuples, the fact that the two Gauss prolongations attached to a place specialisation see the two components of the special fibre of $X_0(Nq)$ at $q$, the Atkin–Lehner involution exchanging them and Kronecker's congruence producing the $q$-power twist. It is used in the construction of local charts and of smooth-locus evaluations for models over the special fibre.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_PlaceSpecialization_ProlongationTuple_residue_jNFun_sub_jNQFun_sub.lean

